  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nModel ensembles have changed little from yesterday's runs showing\nthe peak of the ridge and temperatures occurring on Saturday \nbefore the ridge weakens Sunday into Monday. NBM foreast highs\nhave come down a degree or so from yesterday, but it is hard to\nfind a good reason why. Forecast confidence remains high that \nsome daily heat records are likely to be broken this weekend with \nsoutheast CA lower deserts reaching 115-120 degrees to 113-117 \ndegrees across the Arizona lower deserts. To give some \nprospective, Phoenix has only reached 116 degrees or higher 18 \ntimes after July 30th, half of which occurred since 2023 and only \nreached 117 degrees or higher 6 times. The probability of KPHX \nhitting at least 116 degrees Friday-Sunday is 20%, 67%, and 35% \nrespectively. For Saturday, the NBM also shows a 20-25% \nprobability of tying the all-time hottest temperature in August of\n118 degrees which occurred August 7, 2025. We will have some \nrelief from the extreme heat at night, more so across the western \ndeserts where lows should still dip into the mid to upper 80s. The\nbulk of the Phoenix metro should have lows only in a 90-95 degree\nrange Friday-Sunday. \n\nAny potential rain chances over the weekend are trending even\nlower with guidance mostly showing any decent moisture advection \nholding off until late Sunday into Monday. Some members still\nshow some improved chances over the eastern Arizona high terrain \nconvection as early as Sunday with potential for outflows into \nthe Phoenix area Sunday evening, but it seems more likely that \nwill not occur until Monday at the earliest. Guidance does agree \non the ridge noticeably weakening by Monday with H5 heights \ndropping to 594-596dm, but the residual heat over the region may \nstill keep extreme heat conditions through Monday. The ridge \ncenter should also begin to drift back to the northeast, closer to\nthe Four Corners area by around next Tuesday. If and when this \nhappens, it should open up portions of the region for better \nmoisture advection out of the southeast. Monsoon storm chances are\nexpected to gradually improve going into the middle of next week,\nwhile NBM forecast highs drop back to within a few degrees of \nnormals.",
